  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with two mid-level low pressure systems \nacross the middle of the nation. The norther low is currently over \nwestern Minnesota/eastern South Dakota with a cold front trailing \nsouth into extreme northwestern Iowa and central/eastern Nebraska. \nThe northern low has been pulling warm, gulf moisture into it. \nShowers and weak thunderstorms have been kicking off along the front \nfor much of the evening, and will continue to do so as it sags south \nacross the region overnight. Expect additional showers to develop in \nassociation with the southern low, which is currently located over \nwestern Kansas and the Oklahoma panhandle. This low will lift to the \nnortheast overnight, bringing in moisture from the Pacific. This \nsystem should start moving into our forecast area by daybreak, \ntapping into gulf moisture later in the day.\n\nRain chances will continue through much of the daytime hours, \neventually tapering of from southwest to northeast after sunset. \nCooler air pushes into the region tomorrow with highs topping out in \nthe low to mid-50s for areas along and north of the I-80 corridor. \nAreas closer to the Nebraska/Kansas/Missouri/Iowa border can expect \nto reach the low to mid-60s. Overnight lows will fall into the upper \n30s for areas north of a line from David City to Norfolk.\n\nWednesday, the low continues to slowly lift to the northeast. \nNorthern Nebraska and extreme northwestern Iowa may get clipped by a \nfew wrap around showers. Breezy conditions set up, particularly for \nnorthern Nebraska and extreme northwestern Iowa, with gust \napproaching 25-30mph in spots.\n\nZonal flow sets up over the region Thursday and Friday as the low \nlifts into the Great Lakes region. A lobe of energy may move across \nthe CWA through the day Thursday, bringing a 20-30% chance of a few \nshowers. Conditions will dry out by Thursday evening, persisting \ninto the weekend. Heading into the weekend, a ridge builds to the \nwest, shifting slightly east. Expect highs to return to the 50s and \n60s. Friday and Saturday morning, low temperatures will fall into \nthe low to mid-30s. Those with agricultural interests will want to \nmonitor the forecast.",
